{"i":0,"result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":{"chunk_id":"73f6bc","wall_time_seconds":0.547775334,"exit_code":0,"original_token_count":2996,"output":"{"author":"Soph","body":"**Blocking: cancellation here persistently wedges the ref.**\n\n`exec.CommandContext`'s default cancel is `Process.Kill()` (SIGKILL), and no `Cancel`/`WaitDelay` is set. `CompareAndSwapRef` holds the transaction *prepared* — git owns `.git/refs/\u003cname\u003e.lock` — across the `git symbolic-ref` fork before committing. The root ctx cancels on SIGINT/SIGTERM (`main.go:44-66`), and the pre-push hook runs under it, so Ctrl-C during `git push` lands in that window.\n\nReproduced on git 2.50.1:\n\n| cancel | `.lock` left | later plain `git update-ref` | txn |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| SIGKILL (current) | **yes** | **fails: cannot lock ref … File exists** | aborted |\n| SIGTERM | no | succeeds | aborted |\n| close stdin | no | succeeds | aborted |\n\nGit has no stale ref-lock recovery — its own message says \"remove the file manually to continue\". The OPF path takes the lock once per ref, so a migration backfill has N windows.\n\nTwo things that may help: **SIGTERM is sufficient** (git's tempfile signal cleanup removes the lock), so `procutil.TerminateOnCancel(cmd)` fixes it in one line with an existing helper. And no variant silently commits — I checked the ref value after each, all three abort — so this is purely lock hygiene, not a spurious write. Closing stdin also works but exits 0, which the Go side would then need to not read as success.","commit_id":"eba0847108dfd5541f013ca30c2e7b04222edbc8","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#discussion_r3912492047","id":3912492047,"in_reply_to_id":null,"line":81,"original_commit_id":"74cff6839a71c8fd1b76f511c66bc33345ab59e0","original_line":76,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/gitrepo/ref_cas.go","review_id":5087696024}
{"author":"Soph","body":"**Blocking: this doesn't delay the push, it aborts it.**\n\n`DeadlineExceeded` → `atomicSetV1Ref` (not `ErrRefCASConflict`, so it falls through to `fmt.Errorf(\"set v1 ref: %w\")`) → `RewriteUnpushedV1WithOPF` → `manual_commit_push.go:164` `return rewriteErr` → non-zero pre-push exit → the user's `git push` fails.\n\nNothing classifies it and nothing retries it: `retryPersistentRefLockContention` sits inside the flock, so it can't retry a failed acquire. And the comment above names \"another writer's potentially expensive checkpoint build\" as the expected holder — so it fires precisely in the scenario it was written for, where previously the push waited and succeeded.\n\nScope: git-branch/v1 only. The refs backend's fail-closed path withholds and returns nil, so it degrades correctly. Also worth noting the 2s bounds only the acquire — the CAS retries after it are unbounded, so it doesn't actually cap the hook's contribution to push latency.\n\nEither drop the bound (a delayed push beats a dead one), or classify `DeadlineExceeded` so v1 withholds instead of aborting.","commit_id":"74cff6839a71c8fd1b76f511c66bc33345ab59e0","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#discussion_r3912492055","id":3912492055,"in_reply_to_id":null,"line":null,"original_commit_id":"74cff6839a71c8fd1b76f511c66bc33345ab59e0","original_line":25,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/persistent_ref_update.go","review_id":5087696024}
{"author":"Soph","body":"This changes the gate from deadline-gated to cancelability-gated, which is the opposite of what the comment being deleted promised: it said a deadline-free ctx \"takes the same blocking kernel path as Acquire, **so existing callers keep their exact behavior**\", and named turn-end condensation as a must-wait caller.\n\nEvery CLI context is cancelable (root ctx, `main.go:44`), so in practice only `context.Background()` now reaches the kernel path. Turn-end condensation now does 25ms `LOCK_NB` polling.\n\nThe part I'd raise loudest is fairness rather than the syscalls: `LOCK_EX` gives you a kernel queue position, poll-and-retry gives you none, so under the N-agents-one-repo workflow a poller can be starved indefinitely. This is a shared primitive affecting every caller, and no commit message mentions the change — worth either scoping it to the callers that need it or making it an explicit, documented decision.","commit_id":"74cff6839a71c8fd1b76f511c66bc33345ab59e0","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#discussion_r3912492063","id":3912492063,"in_reply_to_id":null,"line":null,"original_commit_id":"74cff6839a71c8fd1b76f511c66bc33345ab59e0","original_line":66,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/internal/flock/flock_unix.go","review_id":5087696024}
{"author":"Soph","body":"**This replaces my last finding with an unbounded, uninterruptible wait on the same path — and the flock it waits for is not what makes the fix work.**\n\nThe CLI root context (`main.go:29`) is cancelable but carries no deadline, so `lockFile` takes the blocking `syscall.Flock(LOCK_EX)` branch, which never consults ctx. The holder is `updatePersistentRef`, which keeps this same lock across its whole build-and-CAS loop — up to `shadowRefMaxRetries` = 16 checkpoint-tree rebuilds — and the lock file lives in the git **common** dir, so a sibling worktree contends on the same v1 ref name.\n\nScenario: condensation takes the v1 flock in worktree 1 and starts an expensive build (redaction dominates there). A `git push` in worktree 2 reaches `atomicSetV1Ref` -\u003e `CASPersistentRef` and blocks in the kernel. Ctrl-C prints `Interrupting… press Ctrl-C again to force quit` and does nothing; only a second Ctrl-C escapes. No message explains the stall.\n\nConfirmed: holding the flock and calling `CASPersistentRef` with a `context.WithCancel(context.Background())` — exactly main.go's shape — then cancelling gives `still blocked 3s after cancel`.\n\n**And the flock earns almost nothing.** The condensation path already used native `git update-ref`, so the #2204 lost update came purely from OPF's `CheckAndSetReference`; routing OPF through native CAS is the entire fix. This flock only covers the window between its own acquire and the CAS, but `expectedOld` was read at the start of the rewrite, long before the lock — so a condensation landing mid-rewrite yields a conflict either way. Removing the flock from `CASPersistentRef` leaves all three OPF regression tests passing (`TestOPFV1RewriteDoesNotLoseConcurrentCheckpointUpdate`, `TestOPFCheckpointRefRewriteDoesNotLoseConcurrentCheckpointUpdate`, `TestAtomicSetV1Ref_ReportsActualCASConflictAsMoved`); the only failure is `TestCASPersistentRef_WaitsForHeldFlockWithoutCallerDeadline`, which exists to describe the flock itself.\n\nSuggest dropping it here and relying on native CAS plus `retryPersistentRefLockContention` (16 attempts x ~10-25ms, so bounded well under a second). If it is kept for retry-churn reasons it needs a bound **and** a soft classification, so a timeout leaves the refs queued rather than aborting the user's push, plus a stderr line so the wait is not silent.","commit_id":"fc410717c4124641346cdc83f91bb9a71a2f847f","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#discussion_r3925309134","id":3925309134,"in_reply_to_id":null,"line":null,"original_commit_id":"fc410717c4124641346cdc83f91bb9a71a2f847f","original_line":103,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/persistent_ref_update.go","review_id":5102863171}
{"author":"Soph","body":"**This substring also matches three permanent, non-concurrency failures, and one route is a diagnosis regression against main.**\n\nReproduced on git 2.50.1, all classifying as `conflict=true`:\n\n| planted condition | git stderr |\n| --- | --- |\n| corrupt loose ref | `cannot lock ref 'refs/probe/x': unable to resolve reference 'refs/probe/x': reference broken` |\n| unreadable ref (mode 000) | `… unable to resolve reference 'refs/probe/y': Permission denied` |\n| name is a directory | `… unable to resolve reference 'refs/probe/z'` |\n\nVia `casUpdateRef` these become `ErrShadowRefBusy`, so `updatePersistentRef` rebuilds the checkpoint tree and retries **16 times** on the turn-end hook before failing with \"shadow branch ref busy\" — wrong cause, 16x the work. Via `atomicSetV1Ref` they become `V1RefMovedError`, so a corrupt or unreadable v1 ref aborts the push with a concurrency story. That second one is a regression against main, where `storage.ErrReferenceHasChanged` would not fire for these and the real cause surfaced.\n\n`TestCASPersistentRef_DoesNotClassifyRefNamespaceConflictAsContention` covers the D/F case in the other direction (creating `parent/child` when `parent` exists yields `'…' exists; cannot create '…'` and classifies fine). The direction that breaks is the untested one.\n\nThe genuine \"deleted concurrently\" case has no trailing reason (verified: `unable to resolve reference 'refs/probe/gone'` and nothing more), so requiring the absence of a tail separates the first two rows. For the third, gate `V1RefMovedError` in `atomicSetV1Ref` on the re-read actually differing from `expectedOld` — it already does that re-read to fill `Actual`.","commit_id":"fc410717c4124641346cdc83f91bb9a71a2f847f","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#discussion_r3925309148","id":3925309148,"in_reply_to_id":null,"line":null,"original_commit_id":"fc410717c4124641346cdc83f91bb9a71a2f847f","original_line":237,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/gitrepo/ref_cas.go","review_id":5102863171}
{"author":"Soph","body":"`WaitDelay` is left at zero. Go's docs recommend setting it precisely for a custom `Cancel` that does not kill, because otherwise `Wait` blocks indefinitely on both the process and the stderr copier. `cmd.Stderr` is a `bytes.Buffer`, so os/exec allocates a pipe plus a copier goroutine that `Wait` joins — any grandchild inheriting that pipe holds `Wait` open even after git exits.\n\nA blocking `reference-transaction` hook (git runs it at prepared/committed/aborted) reproduces the same wedge inside the child: git never exits on EOF, `tx.wait()` never returns, and the hook hangs with the transaction prepared and the `.lock` held — with the root context's cancel unable to break it.\n\nPlausible rather than confirmed; I reasoned it from os/exec semantics and git's hook points without staging a blocking hook. A few seconds of `cmd.WaitDelay` covers it.","commit_id":"eba0847108dfd5541f013ca30c2e7b04222edbc8","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#discussion_r3925309156","id":3925309156,"in_reply_to_id":null,"line":100,"original_commit_id":"fc410717c4124641346cdc83f91bb9a71a2f847f","original_line":97,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/gitrepo/ref_cas.go","review_id":5102863171}
{"author":"Soph","body":"Worth adding to the PR description: `casUpdateRef` is the shared entry point, so this reroutes more than the two OPF writers the description names — `casUpdateShadowBranchRef` (`checkpoint/shadow_ref.go`) and every `updatePersistentRef` caller go through it too, which puts it on the per-checkpoint shadow-branch write in the Stop hook.\n\nBenchmarked at 60 iterations on a 400-commit repo: the old single `git update-ref \u003cref\u003e \u003cnew\u003e \u003cold\u003e` fork is 13.4ms/op, the new transaction plus the `symbolic-ref` probe is 22.0ms/op — +8.6ms per ref update, and `updatePersistentRef` can retry up to 16 times. Against the ~3s p50 hook floor that is under 1%, so not a blocker, just an unmentioned scope expansion onto a path this repo watches.\n\nI checked the obvious way to drop the extra fork and it does not work: `symref-verify \u003cref\u003e` alongside `update \u003cref\u003e …` in one transaction fails with `fatal: prepare: multiple updates for ref '…' not allowed` on both backends. So the separate fork is a legitimate workaround, not an oversight.","commit_id":"eba0847108dfd5541f013ca30c2e7b04222edbc8","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#discussion_r3925309164","id":3925309164,"in_reply_to_id":null,"line":26,"original_commit_id":"fc410717c4124641346cdc83f91bb9a71a2f847f","original_line":41,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/persistent_ref_update.go","review_id":5102863171}
"}}}

