{"i":0,"result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":{"chunk_id":"26f241","wall_time_seconds":1.001810542,"session_id":91027,"original_token_count":2514,"output":"{"author":"MuskanPaliwal","body":"Pushed a follow-up in [15427c7b1](https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211/commits/15427c7b100bd55b2c5e8ed5e92402e463c21aae) for the review findings. The ref CAS now lives in one place, uses `--no-deref`, and tells a stale ref apart from a held lock. Persistent writers retry when the ref disappears, and flock waits now stop on cancellation even without a deadline. I also added real loose-ref and reftable contention tests, plus coverage through the public OPF paths.\r\n\r\n`mise run check` passes, including race tests, integration tests, and the deterministic canaries. Ready for another look.","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#issuecomment-5492574776"}
{"author":"Soph","body":"#2131 and #2211 both convert the two OPF call sites to native CAS, through two different APIs. `git merge-tree` on the current heads conflicts in six files, including a modify/delete on `persistent_ref_update.go`, which #2131 removes and this PR extends.\n\nWhat do you want to carry forward?\n","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#issuecomment-5493155727"}
{"author":"MuskanPaliwal","body":"Hey @Soph , My bad, I missed the overlap between my two PRs. I want #2131’s transaction and batching design to win in the long run. Since #2204  is a correctness bug, should we merge #2211  now, then reconcile #2131 ? I’d keep #2131 ’s transaction API, move over the locking behavior, CAS fixes, and OPF tests, and drop `CASPersistentRef` and `persistent_ref_update.go`.\r\n\r\nOr would you rather I move those pieces into #2131  now and close #2211 ? I outlined the design in [my latest #2131 comment](https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2131#issuecomment-5489979780), and I’d appreciate a review before I start moving code.","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#issuecomment-5493827846"}
{"author":"Soph","body":"Agreed on the order: land this one, reconcile #2131 on top. #2204 is a lost update and #2131 is a 3,900 line refactor with Stage 3 still to write, so I would rather not couple them. Your reconcile plan is the right shape, and #2131's OPF hunks become no-ops once this lands.\n\nRe-reviewed at `f342d4fbd`. Both earlier findings are fixed, verified rather than taken from the description. One thing to fix before merge.\n\n`ErrRefLocked` is split out because lock contention does not prove the ref moved, and then treated as terminal anyway. `atomicSetV1Ref` maps only `ErrRefCASConflict`; everything else falls through to `set v1 ref: %w`, which `manual_commit_push.go:164-170` returns, so the user's `git push` fails. `manual_commit_opf_refs.go:169` has the same shape via `warnOPFCheckpointRefsWithheld`. Every other CAS caller in this call graph does retry it: `persistent_ref_update.go:118-127` and both shadow loops build `ErrShadowRefBusy` from both sentinels and retry 16 times.\n\nThis is reachable from Entire alone on reftable repos, because reftable's ref lock is repo-global rather than per-ref. A prepared transaction on a shadow branch blocks a CAS on the unrelated v1 ref (git 2.50.1):\n\n```\n--- shadow-branch txn prepared; now CAS the UNRELATED v1 ref ---\nfatal: update_ref failed for ref 'refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1': cannot lock references\n```\n\nBoth sides there are our own native `update-ref`: a TurnEnd shadow write against the pre-push OPF rewrite. The shadow writer retries and survives; the OPF writer aborts the push and discards the rewrite. Loose-ref repos are much narrower, and `packed-refs.lock` does not widen it, since a held `packed-refs.lock` blocks deletions only and updates always land loose.\n\nThe fix is to give the two OPF CAS sites the same bounded retry the persistent path has, on `ErrRefLocked` only. `ErrRefCASConflict` should stay terminal there, since a moved ref really does invalidate the rewrite.\n","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#issuecomment-5494992508"}
{"author":"MuskanPaliwal","body":"Hey @Soph , kindly re-review. Thanks :))","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#issuecomment-5495480794"}
{"author":"Soph","body":"Ran a security review over this. The main question, whether the new classification can let un-redacted content reach the remote, came back clean: git's actual stderr was measured for eight failure modes against both the files and reftable backends, every one still yields a non-nil error, and every caller either aborts the push or withholds the flush with the refs left queued. Nothing fails open. Two other things came out of it.\n\n**The v1 CAS now waits unboundedly on the user's push path.** `atomicSetV1Ref` took no flock before this PR; it now goes through `CASPersistentRef` into `withPersistentRefFlock`, and the hook's root context is `context.WithCancel(context.Background())` (`main.go:29`), so there is no deadline to bound the wait. `GitStore.updatePrimaryRef` holds that same lock across its entire build closure, condensation included, so a turn-end hook running concurrently stalls `git push` for as long as condensation takes. Measured: with the lock held from another fd, `atomicSetV1Ref` was still blocked at 3s and returned in 3.017s the instant it was released. Ctrl-C does interrupt it, which the old kernel-blocking path would not have, so this is not a wedge. Still, a bounded acquire that fails with a clear message reads better here than an indefinite wait, given what is on the other side of it is the user's push.\n\n**`--no-deref` destroys a symbolic ref, and the test pins that as intended.** `git update-ref --no-deref` compares `\u003coldvalue\u003e` against the resolved hash but writes the symref itself, so the CAS succeeds and the symbolic ref is replaced by a hash reference; against `HEAD` that detaches it. The go-git call this replaced returned `reference has changed concurrently` and left the symref intact, so this direction fails open where the old one failed closed. `TestCASPersistentRef_DoesNotDereferenceSymbolicRef` asserts `require.NoError` and then `require.Equal(t, plumbing.HashReference, updated.Type())`, which locks in the replacement, while the name reads as protection against it. No current caller passes a symbolic ref, so this is latent rather than exploitable. I would still rather `CompareAndSwapRef` rejected a symbolic target than have a test asserting it is handled.\n","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#issuecomment-5498149722"}
{"author":"MuskanPaliwal","body":"Thanks @Soph  for digging into this. Fixed both issues in [`462fd2d2c`](https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211/commits/462fd2d2c44fc22b0c0d09405fe4677302d78772).\r\n\r\n`CASPersistentRef` now waits up to two seconds for Entire's writer lock, then returns a clear timeout. The timeout only covers lock acquisition. Once acquired, the CAS uses the caller's original context.\r\n\r\nSymbolic refs are now rejected while Git holds the ref lock. The code prepares an `update-ref` transaction, checks the ref type, then aborts for a symbolic ref or commits for a direct ref. A competing Git process cannot change the ref type between the check and the write. Ref names are also validated before they enter the stdin protocol.\r\n\r\nI tested this with files and reftable repositories. Symbolic refs and their targets stay unchanged, a concurrent `git symbolic-ref` fails while the transaction is prepared, and a held Entire lock times out around two seconds without moving the ref. I also exercised eight failure cases on each backend; all returned errors and left the refs unchanged.\r\n\r\n`mise run check` passes, including the integration race suite and canaries.\r\n","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#issuecomment-5499526462"}
{"author":"MuskanPaliwal","body":"Hi @Soph, let me know if this pr is good to go. Thanks :))","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#issuecomment-5506492520"}
{"author":"MuskanPaliwal","body":"Thanks @Soph for being patient with me and for the detailed review. I fixed the latest findings in  [82b12d2](https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211/commits/82b12d2e32f47bccc254194ea5e0dc3aecb38bc9). Cancellation now aborts prepared ref transactions without leaving stale locks, OPF CAS waits instead of failing the user’s push, and Git commands ignore inherited repo overrides. I also restored kernel lock fairness and added loose-ref and reftable regression tests. `mise run check` passes.","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#issuecomment-5507943149"}
{"author":"MuskanPaliwal","body":"Hey @Soph , highly appreciate your feedback here. The PR looks a lot cleaner now. Thanks :))","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#issuecomment-5513085747"}
